Link wrote:

 > The problem is that I don't think learning how to
 > survive the early game while underleveled really
 > helps with learning how to survive the rest of the
 > game while not underleveled.

Well, you're ignoring that the "underleveled" PC
will still see those nastier monsters, just later in
the game, when better equipped.

 > For starters, a lot of the early deaths I see
 > broadcasted by Rodney are due to ants and mumaks
 > -- and I don't think the starting game monsters
 > are in anyway comparable to ants or mumaks --
 > regardless of what character level you are.

Right, but those "early deaths by ants and mumaks"
exist _precisely_ because PCs are so highly leveled
up that ants and mumaks DO occur in the early game,
before the PC is equipped to handle them.

Stay below the XL that puts those monsters into the
_early_ game, and your PC's survival chances against
them are much better at later, deferred meetings.

I just checked my logfile for deaths by giant or
soldier ants and the total is a bit under 5% of all
deaths (33/672), but highly skewed, 31 of them
are in the 2/3rds of my games before I started
playing purely protection racket, and only 2 of them
in the 1/3rd of my games (in the now over two
calendar years) since.

It isn't that my PC doesn't eventually meet ants,
its just that when met, they are now pretty much
popcorn monsters for my PC.

 > Another giant hurdle, which is dealing with swarms
 > of monsters jumping into a polymorph trap, would
 > never be seen in a protection racket either.

So? It would still be seen when the PC goes below
mines town, just as for PCs rushing to that battle
without the added equippage and AC the protection
racket affords.

It isn't the claim that the protection racket is
going to train the player for ALL monster situations
EVERYWHERE, it sure isn't doing someone like me who
rarely survives to see the Big Room much good in
understanding how to fight The Riders on their own
turf, for example.

It's just that the protection racket keeps the
player's mind wonderfully focused on "you don't
_have to_ MELEE what you cannot kill, seek
alternatives". That wonderfully trained thought
process, in concentrated form in the protection
racket because it is an absolutely survival
requirement, serves _in general_ throughout the
game, even though for some of us that's a rather
abbreviated "Rest of the Story".

xanthian.

Nor will it do anything wonderful about countering
sheer bad luck, as in my recent game where I got to
the top level of the Sokoban version with entrance
on the bottom left and really confined, only to find
one of the giant mimics standing in the square next
to the stairs.  Instantly dead and all, when my pet
woke the giant mimic, I grumbled mostly about dying
where I couldn't leave a bones file.



